course: religion-ethics
lesson: 26
title: The church year
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Christians do not only tell the story of Jesus once; they walk through it every year, season by season, in what is called the church year.
explain: It begins with Advent, four weeks of getting ready, then Christmas for Jesus' birth and Epiphany for the wise men who came from far away.
explain: Pancake Day comes just before Lent, forty days of self-control; Holy Week follows Jesus through Palm Sunday, the Last Supper and Good Friday, and Easter Day celebrates him rising to new life.
explain: Many churches change the colours of their cloths with the seasons, for example purple for Advent and Lent, white or gold for Christmas and Easter, and green for the ordinary weeks.
explain: For a child today the church year is a calendar that teaches: waiting, giving, saying sorry, thanking God at harvest and learning from saints all come round again each year.
fact: The church year is the cycle of seasons Christians follow each year to tell the story of Jesus.
fact: Advent is the four weeks before Christmas, a time of waiting and getting ready, with Advent wreaths and calendars.
fact: Christmas is on 25 December, but many Orthodox churches celebrate it on 7 January because they use an older calendar.
fact: Epiphany, on 6 January, remembers the wise men's visit and Jesus being shown to the whole world.
fact: Shrove Tuesday, or Pancake Day, is the day before Lent, when people used up rich foods such as eggs and butter.
fact: Lent is forty days before Easter remembering Jesus' forty days in the desert; it begins on Ash Wednesday.
fact: On Ash Wednesday some Christians receive a cross of ashes on their forehead as a sign of saying sorry to God.
fact: In Lent many Christians give something up or take something good on.
fact: Holy Week runs from Palm Sunday through Maundy Thursday, which remembers the Last Supper, and Good Friday to Easter Day.
fact: Easter Day is the greatest Christian festival, celebrating Jesus rising from the dead; eggs are a sign of new life.
fact: Harvest Festival is an autumn service thanking God for food, and the food brought is given to people in need.
fact: Thanksgiving in North America has a spirit similar to Harvest Festival.
fact: St Patrick, St David, St Andrew and St George are the patron saints of Ireland, Wales, Scotland and England; a patron saint is a saint seen as the special friend and helper of a country, and people keep his day.
fact: Many churches use purple in Advent and Lent, white or gold at Christmas and Easter, and green in ordinary weeks.
exercise: What is the church year? | The cycle of seasons that tells the story of Jesus each year | The year a church building was first opened | The school year for the children at Sunday school, from September | The number of years a vicar stays in one church
exercise: What is Advent? | Four weeks of waiting and getting ready before Christmas | The forty days of fasting and saying sorry before Easter Day | The week after Easter when eggs are eaten | The autumn service thanking God for food
exercise: Which colour do many churches use in Advent and Lent? | Purple | Green | Red | Yellow
exercise: On which date do many Orthodox churches celebrate Christmas? | 7 January | 25 December | 6 January | 1 January
exercise: What does Epiphany remember? | The wise men's visit and Jesus shown to the world | The angel's visit to Mary in Nazareth | Jesus' baptism by John in the river | Jesus riding into Jerusalem on a donkey
exercise: Why did people make pancakes on Shrove Tuesday? | To use up rich foods like eggs and butter before Lent | To celebrate the end of the forty days of Lent | To remember the bread that Jesus broke with his friends at the Last Supper | To thank God for the wheat harvest in autumn
exercise: What does Lent remember? | Jesus' forty days in the desert | The forty years in the wilderness | Jesus' forty years as a carpenter | The forty days of rain in Noah's flood
exercise: What happens on Ash Wednesday in some churches? | People receive a cross of ashes on their forehead | People eat pancakes together after the morning service | People bring food for the harvest table | People wave palm branches down the street
exercise: What do many Christians do during Lent? | Give something up or take something good on | Give each other chocolate eggs every single day | Stay away from church altogether for forty days | Light one candle each Sunday on a green wreath
exercise: Which day comes first in Holy Week? | Palm Sunday | Good Friday | Maundy Thursday | Easter Day
exercise: What is the greatest Christian festival? | Easter Day | Christmas Day | Harvest Festival | Pancake Day
exercise: What do Easter eggs stand for? | New life | Rich food | The wise men's gifts | The harvest
exercise: What happens to the food brought to a Harvest Festival? | It is given to people in need | It is kept for the vicar's family | It is thrown away after the service | It is sold to pay for the church roof
exercise: Which festival in North America has a spirit like Harvest Festival? | Thanksgiving | Halloween | Independence Day | Valentine's Day
exercise: Who is the patron saint of Wales? | St David | St Patrick | St Andrew | St George
exercise: Why do Christians keep saints' days? | Good examples help them live well | Saints asked for days off work | To have more days to eat pancakes | Because saints were born on Sundays
test: my little bro says Advent is the forty days before Easter. right? | No, Advent is the four weeks before Christmas | Yes, Advent and Lent are the same season | Yes, but only in Orthodox churches in Greece and Russia | No, Advent is the week after Easter
test: Olga from class says her family has Christmas on 7 January. Is she mixing it up? | No, many Orthodox churches keep Christmas on 7 January | Yes, every church keeps Christmas on 25 December | Yes, 7 January is the day of Epiphany | No, 7 January is when Advent begins
test: why's it called pancake day anyway | People used up eggs and butter the day before Lent | Jesus ate pancakes at the Last Supper | The wise men brought pancakes to Jesus | Pancakes stand for the round crown of thorns
test: Sam got ashes on his head at church and his mate said "you've got muck on ur face lol". What was it? | A cross of ashes for Ash Wednesday, a sign of saying sorry | A mark from Palm Sunday showing that he had waved a palm branch | A sign that he has been baptised that morning | A mark you get at Harvest for bringing food
test: Noah says Good Friday is good cos Jesus rose from the dead that day. Fix it. | Jesus died on Good Friday and rose on Easter Day | Jesus was born on Good Friday, and was not raised from the dead then | Jesus rose on Palm Sunday, a week earlier | Jesus rose on Maundy Thursday at the supper
test: A friend with no religion asks: "what's the point of Easter eggs, just chocolate innit?" | Eggs are a sign of new life, like Jesus rising | Eggs show how rich the church was in the past | Eggs remind people of the wise men's gifts | Eggs stand for the rich food eaten before Lent
test: The church cloth has gone green and nothing special is happening. What season is it most likely? | The ordinary weeks between the big seasons | Advent, the time of getting ready | Lent, the time of saying sorry | Easter, the greatest festival
test: shut up about pancakes, what are u actually giving up for lent | Lent is about self-control and making room for God and others | Lent is about eating as much as you can before Easter | Lent is about decorating the house for Christmas | Lent is about bringing food to the harvest service
test: Your sis puts the wise men in the crib on Christmas Eve. When does the church year actually remember their visit? | On Epiphany, 6 January | On Palm Sunday in spring | On the first Sunday of Advent | On Easter Day
test: Grace says Harvest is just for farmers, nothing to do with us townies. Best answer? | Everyone eats food, so everyone can thank God and share it | She is right, only farmers should give thanks | Harvest is only for people who grow their own food in a garden or on a farm | Town churches do not keep Harvest Festival at all
test: The judge asks: "Why might a calendar teach faith better than one lesson?" | Each season repeats the story and its lessons every year | A calendar has pictures, which are always better than words | Children only learn things that happen in winter | Lessons are forbidden in church, so calendars replace them
test: Which saint's day would a family in Scotland most likely celebrate as their own? | St Andrew's Day | St Patrick's Day | St David's Day | St George's Day
exam: Identify the season of the ecclesiastical year comprising the four weeks preceding the Nativity. | Advent | Lent | Epiphany | Holy Week
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the festival accounted the greatest in the Christian calendar. | Easter Day, celebrating the Resurrection | Christmas Day, celebrating the Nativity | Harvest Festival, celebrating the crops | Epiphany, celebrating the Magi
exam: Ascertain the purpose originally served by the consumption of pancakes upon Shrove Tuesday. | The using up of rich foods prior to Lent | The commemoration of the Last Supper | The celebration of the conclusion of Lent | The thanksgiving for the gathered harvest
exam: Whereas certain Orthodox churches observe the Nativity upon a later date, state the date in question. | The seventh of January | The sixth of January | The first of January | The twenty-fifth of December
